## Pipeline

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Ticket in] --> B[OpenAI classifier<br/>Zod-validated]
  B --> C[Rules engine<br/>ordered match]
  C --> D[(Postgres)]
  C --> E{Fraud or<br/>escalation?}
  E -- yes --> F[Slack delivery<br/>Block Kit]
  E -- no --> G[Queue only]
  F --> D
```

## Why this exists

Most SaaS support inboxes get a flood of tickets, but only ~5% are actually urgent — fraud reports, production outages, suspicious logins. Today, that 5% gets surfaced by a human scanning the queue, which adds 15–60 minutes of latency. PagerDuty-style integrations exist, but they trigger off infrastructure metrics, not the *content* of customer messages. This system classifies every incoming ticket and pipes the urgent ones into Slack so engineering and security see them in seconds.

The bigger story is architectural. Most "AI automation" demos let the LLM do everything — classify, route, *and* fire side-effects — which makes the system non-deterministic and untestable. This one splits those jobs by design: the LLM classifies, a deterministic rules engine routes, a separate delivery layer fires the external action. Each boundary is enforced by the type system, not just a system prompt.

## What's built

- **AI classifier.** OpenAI gpt-4o-mini with `response_format: json_object` and temperature 0.1. Output is Zod-validated; any failure (network, malformed JSON, schema mismatch) drops to a safe `FALLBACK_CLASSIFICATION` so the rules engine never sees garbage.
- **Rules engine.** Pure TypeScript, ordered rule list. Six rules plus a fallback. Fraud sits above the high-priority short-circuit on purpose — a fraud + high-priority case still goes to the fraud team, not generic escalation.
- **Slack delivery layer.** When the rules engine routes to `fraud` or `escalation`, the pipeline posts a Block Kit message to a configured Slack webhook with category, priority, intent, confidence, the original input quote, and a link back to the dashboard. Other routes stay queue-only. Failures (Slack down, webhook revoked) are appended to the workflow's action log without breaking the rest of the pipeline.
- **Synthetic SaaS dataset.** A seeded faker generator produces 600 historical workflows with realistic shape — time-of-day weighting in business hours, mixed categories, a believable status distribution, and synthetic Slack delivery outcomes on the fraud/escalation rows.
- **REST API.** `POST /api/ingest`, `GET /api/workflows` (with filters via Postgres jsonb path queries), `GET /api/workflows/[id]`, `GET /api/insights`. Each route is a thin Next 16 route handler over a shared pipeline function.
- **Dashboard.** Sticky sidebar with status filters, four KPIs, an AI insights panel, an interactive ingest form that runs the full classify → route → deliver pipeline live, and an expandable workflow table with Slack delivery status per row.

## Technical choices

### "Classify, route, deliver" — enforced by types

The rules engine takes a typed `Classification` and returns a typed `RoutingDecision`. The Slack delivery layer takes that and returns a typed `ActionEntry`. There is no codepath where the LLM produces a routing target or fires an external call. The system prompt also enforces the boundary in language ("classify only — do NOT route, respond, or solve") but the type system is the actual guarantee.

### Validate at the boundary, fall back inside

The classifier exposes `parseClassification(raw)` as a separate pure function — no API call, no side-effects, just JSON.parse + Zod. The smoke suite drives seven malformed inputs through it directly to assert each one falls through to the safe fallback. The Slack layer uses the same pattern: a non-2xx or thrown fetch becomes `slack_failed` on the action log, the workflow itself still completes.

### Workflow record IS the audit trail

No separate event log table. Each workflow row carries its full lifecycle in one record: input, classification, routing decision, matched rule, action log (including Slack delivery outcome), status, durations, error message.

### Deterministic seed → testable engine

`mulberry32(1337)` plus `faker.seed(1337)` make the dataset byte-reproducible. The smoke script asserts exact rule-ordering invariants — nine cases including "fraud beats high priority" and "billing+refund routes to finance, not just billing."

## Stack

Next.js 16 App Router · MUI v9 · Prisma + Neon Postgres · OpenAI gpt-4o-mini · Zod for I/O validation · Slack incoming webhooks · `@faker-js/faker` · TypeScript end-to-end.
